# Other CSS Selectors

I consider the selectors we have learned so far to be the **essential** ones: they cover most of what a page needs. But CSS has many more selectors, some of which can be very useful. In this reading, I will focus on two groups: **pseudo-classes** and more **combinators** (beyond [compound selectors](<07 - Combining Selectors.webnb#compound-selectors>), [descendant selectors](<07 - Combining Selectors.webnb#descendant-selectors>), and [selector lists](<07 - Combining Selectors.webnb#selector-lists>), which we covered earlier).

## Pseudo-Classes

A **pseudo-class** describes a state an element (rather than something written in HTML). It attaches to a selector after a colon.

### `:hover`

`:hover` matches while the mouse pointer is over the element. Move your pointer over the button below:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      button {
        font-size: 16px;
        padding: 8px 16px;
      }
      button:hover {
        background-color: gold;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <button>Hover over me</button>
  </body>
</html>
```

Nothing about the button's HTML changed. The browser applies the second rule while the pointer is inside it and drops it when the pointer leaves.

### `:focus`

`:focus` matches while the element has keyboard focus, which is where typing would go. Click into the field below, or press Tab until it is selected:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      input:focus {
        outline: 3px solid red;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <input placeholder="Click here, or press Tab">
  </body>
</html>
```

This one is worth taking seriously rather than treating as decoration. Somebody navigating with the keyboard has no pointer to tell them where they are, and a visible focus style is the only thing showing them.

### `:first-child` and `:nth-child()`

These match on **position among siblings**. `:first-child` matches an element that is the first child of its parent. `:nth-child()` takes a value in parentheses and matches accordingly: a number for one position, or a keyword like `even` or `odd`, which is the usual way to get striped rows:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      .examples li:first-child {
        color: green;
      }
      .examples li:nth-child(even) {
        background-color: lightyellow;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <ul class="examples">
      <li>First item</li>
      <li>Second item</li>
      <li>Third item</li>
      <li>Fourth item</li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>
```

The first item is green, and every second item has a pale background. Add another `<li>` to the list and the striping extends to it without any change to the CSS, which is the reason to write it this way instead of adding a class to every other row.

### `:not()` and `:has()`

`:not()` matches elements that do **not** match the selector inside it. `:has()` matches elements that **contain** something matching the selector inside it:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      .notes p:not(.special) {
        opacity: 0.4;
      }
      section:has(h2) {
        background-color: lavender;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="notes">
      <p class="special">This paragraph is special.</p>
      <p>This one is not.</p>
      <p>Neither is this one.</p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>This section has a heading</h2>
      <p>So the section gets a background.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p>This section has no heading, so it does not.</p>
    </section>
  </body>
</html>
```

`:has()` is a little different from other selectors we have written, as it looks at what is *inside* an element to decide how the element itself should look.

[There are many more pseudo-classes](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Pseudo-classes), including `:active` (while a button or link is being pressed), `:visited` (a link the user has already followed), and `:last-child`.

## More Combinators

The descendant selector (`A B`, with a space) matches a `B` anywhere inside an `A`. Three more combinators say something more specific about the relationship.

**The child combinator, `A > B`,** matches a `B` that is a *direct* child of an `A`, and not one nested more deeply:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      .parent > span {
        background-color: lightblue;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="parent">
      <span>A direct child of the div.</span>
      <p>A paragraph holding <span>a span nested one level deeper.</span></p>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
```

Only the first span is highlighted. Change the `>` to a space and run the cell again: both spans match, because both are descendants.

**The adjacent sibling combinator, `A + B`,** matches a `B` that comes immediately after an `A`, with the same parent.

**The subsequent sibling combinator, `A ~ B`,** matches every `B` that comes after an `A` under the same parent, immediately or not:

```{html autorun}
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      h3 + p {
        font-weight: bold;
      }
      h3 ~ p {
        color: darkblue;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p>Before the heading.</p>
    <h3>A heading</h3>
    <p>Immediately after the heading.</p>
    <p>Later, still after the heading.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

Both paragraphs after the heading turn dark blue, because `~` reaches all of them. Only the one directly after it is bold, because `+` reaches exactly one. The paragraph before the heading is untouched by either: these combinators only look forward.

## Exercises

1. Make the list stripe: give every even-numbered `<li>` a `lightyellow` background using `:nth-child()`.

    ```html id=page1
    <ul>
      <li>One</li>
      <li>Two</li>
      <li>Three</li>
      <li>Four</li>
    </ul>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your CSS here */
    ```
    ```+solution
    li:nth-child(even) {
      background-color: lightyellow;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page1
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('li:nth-child(2)').should('have.css', 'background-color: lightyellow').run('The second item should have a lightyellow background. Try `li:nth-child(even)`', 'Striped the even items');
    assert('li:nth-child(4)').should('have.css', 'background-color: lightyellow').run('Every even item should be striped, not only the second one', 'Striped every even item');
    assert('li:nth-child(1)').should('not.have.css', 'background-color: lightyellow').run('The odd-numbered items should be left alone', 'Left the odd items alone');
    ```

2. Use `:hover` to turn the button's `background-color` `gold` while the pointer is over it, and leave it alone otherwise.

    ```html id=page2
    <button>Press me</button>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your CSS here */
    ```
    ```+solution
    button:hover {
      background-color: gold;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page2
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(/button\s*:\s*hover/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Attach the `:hover` pseudo-class to the `button` selector', 'Used the :hover pseudo-class');
    wrap(/background-color\s*:\s*gold/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Set `background-color` to `gold` inside the `:hover` rule', 'Set the hover background');
    assert('button').should('not.have.css', 'background-color: gold').run('The button should only be gold while the pointer is over it, so put the declaration inside the `:hover` rule rather than the plain `button` rule', 'The plain button is unstyled');
    ```

3. Use the child combinator to give a `lightblue` background to spans that are direct children of `.parent`, leaving the more deeply nested span alone.

    ```html id=page3
    <div class="parent">
      <span>Direct child</span>
      <p>Paragraph with <span>a nested span</span></p>
    </div>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your CSS here */
    ```
    ```+solution
    .parent > span {
      background-color: lightblue;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('.parent > span').should('have.css', 'background-color: lightblue').run('The direct child span should have a lightblue background', 'Styled the direct child');
    assert('.parent p span').should('not.have.css', 'background-color: lightblue').run('The nested span should be left alone. A space matches any descendant; `>` matches only a direct child', 'Left the nested span alone');
    ```

4. Reading `:has()`

    ```{mcq}
    Which elements does `section:has(h2)` select?
    correct: Correct! `:has()` selects the element itself, based on what it contains. It is the one selector that looks inside an element to decide how that element should look.
    wrong: Read the selector out loud: "a section that has an h2". Which part of that is the thing being styled?
    [x] Every `<section>` that contains an `<h2>`
    [ ] Every `<h2>` that is inside a `<section>`
    [ ] Every `<section>` that comes immediately after an `<h2>`
    [ ] Every `<h2>` and, separately, every `<section>`
    ```

5. What is the difference between `+` and `~`?

    ```{mcq}
    A page has an `<h3>` followed by three `<p>` elements. How many does `h3 + p` style, and how many does `h3 ~ p` style?
    correct: Correct! `+` matches only the sibling immediately after, so one paragraph. `~` matches every later sibling, so all three.
    wrong: One combinator reaches exactly one element and the other reaches all the following siblings. Look back at "More Combinators".
    [x] `+` styles one, `~` styles all three
    [ ] `+` styles all three, `~` styles one
    [ ] Both style all three
    [ ] Both style only the first
    ```

